update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('8661f25d-6939-4072-9f3f-5c6a8c8de525'::uuid,'898f1418ee0fd1ff246904546d366217','An example would be one of the actual warnings the conclusion is about, such as a scare over a particular food. Fire drills aren''t health warnings at all. The physician isn''t listing a warning; the physician is comparing a different situation that works the same way.'),
('3fe81613-9747-414f-88e2-79590bfbef3c'::uuid,'1e27394fc10e73e5a25762ca65c460ed','"After all" tells us the fire drill sentence is a reason for the conclusion, so it can''t play "no logical role". The physician also never questions the medical establishment''s motives. The complaint is about the strategy of announcing tentative results, not about why the establishment does it.'),
('69ddbb44-f757-4cca-a093-b30747ae1d5c'::uuid,'7befb13002a04c40edbf00c4dc18f080','This matches the prediction in both parts. "After all" makes the sentence support for the claim before it, which is the conclusion. And because fire drills come from a different area of life but follow the same pattern (repeated signals, little real danger, people tune out), the support works as an analogy.

The move for the next Role question: first decide support, conclusion, or opposing view using the indicator words. Then check the description of the claim. Here both halves of the answer had to hold, and they do.'),
('58b22f57-d877-4108-be7e-8bd645d05f65'::uuid,'9e09db0eeebe4ffa2ddcbb35e98fa58f','An objection would have to make the conclusion less likely, and there''s no opposing view anywhere in this argument. The fire drill line pushes the same way the physician does: it explains why constant warnings backfire, which is exactly why the physician wants fewer, conclusive ones.'),
('2c38e082-ad21-4901-bfb5-5482a206f718'::uuid,'d6acc42d0aac31d8ee673c36eefe150b','The physician does mention tentative conclusions and definitive studies, but the fire drill line doesn''t explain how those two differ. Remove it and the difference between a tentative result and a conclusive one is just as clear. Its job is to show what too many warnings do to people.'),
('868d95f4-cdb2-498f-a353-afb2032be832'::uuid,'3bdc72e69d1fa0a9d422e6126d232576','This connects practice to automatic handwriting. That just tells us how kids became automatic. Composition never appears, so it does nothing to show that automatic writing frees up mental resources for other activities.'),
('8de26de0-5100-4c55-a58d-78fe0c824b6b'::uuid,'40075b23dc286dd4527b3a29db1f39e6','This one tempts because it links automatic writing with composition gains. But it is about kids who were already automatic before the lessons, and that fits both stories equally well: automatic writers had attention free, or they were simply stronger students who would improve at composition anyway. An answer that fits the rival as well as the author''s story can''t favor the author. B never shows that becoming more automatic did anything.'),
('3624e49f-9063-49d2-8fcb-50ed4271de99'::uuid,'b991cd7d590afd8263a66f66285175c3','This is the prediction. The evidence only said the best improvers ended up most automatic. C says the kids whose automaticity improved more also generally improved more at composition. Now the gain in one skill tracks the gain in the other during the same lessons, which is what the author''s story predicts and what the stronger-students story doesn''t need.

The move: when a study''s evidence compares a starting trait with an improvement, look for an answer that turns it into change matching change.'),
('c418baf9-e59b-4f87-ba57-e884d04fed75'::uuid,'c352edc961aec44e1215a10bd99fc480','A representative group helps us apply the results to first-graders in general. It doesn''t touch the real question, which is whether automatic handwriting causes the composition gains or something else explains both.'),
('534f119d-af5b-4035-8d4c-025fc5b19211'::uuid,'f8f7d5c2f20ebb9fbb3f39862f020873','This runs the other way. Kids who started with strong composition skills became much more automatic, which hints that composition skill (or general ability) drives automaticity. That''s a rival story, not support for the author''s one.'),
('63ff81ea-97b3-4c18-979e-d96f1723e02f'::uuid,'88a2703d61c4e2af086c8a60caddea06','If M can be made as a liquid, that adds a fourth option outside tablet, capsule, and soft-gel. That makes capsule less certain, not more. It also does nothing to show that M tastes unpleasant, so the three-option rule still isn''t triggered.'),
('47fe78f1-cd86-492d-b1cf-bb1b3decbfd2'::uuid,'dffd1b91054d0beee4897261cbe194f1','This is the missing trigger. With M tasting unpleasant, the first premise applies: M will generally come as a tablet, capsule, or soft-gel. The heat rules out tablet, the company''s missing technology rules out soft-gel, and capsule is what remains.
PT → (T or C or SG)
PT, ~T, ~SG, so C

The move: when an argument eliminates options from a rule, check that the rule actually applies to the thing in the conclusion.'),
('d1cd2901-904c-49c5-853a-013b60d5475a'::uuid,'9cdf3da85555d76a2721a9a736ca3685','This says a capsule medication isn''t also a soft-gel (C → ~SG). We already know M won''t be a soft-gel, so this adds nothing. And it still doesn''t tell us M is limited to the three forms, because nothing says M tastes unpleasant.'),
('242195a2-95b6-484f-b56e-d57bc5cb503b'::uuid,'a2695e7ccd8cd99e3ed3846dd7d56d21','If most low-melting-point medications are soft-gels, that points M toward a form this company can''t make. It gives no reason to think M ends up as a capsule, and it never puts M under the unpleasant-taste rule.'),
('ef879f8c-59b5-47d1-a3e0-9cfd7646a061'::uuid,'0f9d42891ed6de471356c5f62d2c15f2','This gives a reason to choose capsules for bad-tasting medicine, but it only matters if M tastes bad. E never says it does. Without that, M isn''t covered by the rule and could be produced in a form outside the three listed.'),
('7b8d9c65-a58b-44b2-8b13-98706523139c'::uuid,'2cf8110703df411ee4d0b5105dd14da0','Personality isn''t morality. If jurors went by personality, the speeches would show what personalities jurors liked, not what citizens believed was moral. This connects impressing jurors to the wrong thing.'),
('b319dbea-feee-4540-a0a7-8a051c0ec03a'::uuid,'40899263ae2c58448dc6ba8412789f8c','This says jurors scrutinized moral codes more closely than other people did. Close scrutiny doesn''t tell us what jurors wanted to hear. Litigants might respond by presenting an idealized code instead of the common one, so the speeches still might not reflect ordinary moral views.'),
('06a3f8e6-2f35-49bc-a4e8-307ae3a9abb9'::uuid,'b9c77fba4cb62b2a1e4152d8cadc2aa8','This is the link we predicted. If litigants believed jurors were impressed by a "professed moral code" that resembled their own, litigants would shape their speeches to echo the jurors'' morals. Jurors were ordinary citizens, so the speeches would reflect common moral views.

The move: when the evidence is about one goal (impress jurors) and the conclusion is about something else (morality), look for the answer that ties the goal to the conclusion''s subject.'),
('c7246c7a-a437-4118-9c5a-8dc501b22b9b'::uuid,'9847b1f5573ba322f3d897244cefefec','If jurors favored litigants of their own economic class, litigants would pitch their speeches to class, not to shared moral views. The oratory would then show what appealed to the jurors'' class, which points away from the conclusion.'),
('98164b7a-ba2c-4354-b7f6-a9cad2f20557'::uuid,'4e2f4a270a500e5996bbc2ee710f1e7d','The argument says jurors "received no guidance on points of law". If litigants still expected jurors to decide on a good understanding of the law, they would argue the law rather than appeal to morals. The speeches would then tell us less about common moral views.'),
('d540d41c-5d8b-42a1-9a4d-e3be97e07978'::uuid,'1061e139634e6cd24aee8c4c4f2fa89c','This matches the mechanism. Burrowing termites work underground, and here the grass dies from the roots only, with no damage above. It also describes newly forming circles, so the damage happens as the circle appears, which cuts against termites showing up afterward. It doesn''t prove the hypothesis, but it makes the burrowing story fit the facts much better.

The move: when a hypothesis names a specific mechanism, look for evidence that the effect shows that mechanism''s fingerprint.'),
('4e806131-402b-4ff5-98b5-a4efa3fdc276'::uuid,'4ba87b7a87c5fb148c90f448cc0bc69a','This is about the grasses around the circles, not inside them. At most it suggests drought isn''t what kills those grasses, which removes one rival. But it says nothing about what kills the plants that do die, and dropping one rival doesn''t point to termites over every other cause.'),
('196b41ce-3a18-49e4-bca3-bf43f98b204c'::uuid,'a8f2e735083566bf988556c2c80bf1b2','Wetter soil inside the circles doesn''t point to burrowing. It could just be an effect of the bare ground: no plants are drinking the water. It could even attract termites to circles that formed some other way, which is the reverse direction the argument needs to avoid.'),
('6e724bd5-ff7e-4170-a32f-3cccdaaf74d6'::uuid,'e1078ba703b31ba9bb3f051be11a2ad5','Circles forming near other circles fits almost any local cause: the soil, the water table, or termites. Since it is equally consistent with every story, it doesn''t make the termite story more likely.'),
('a7b0f695-c43c-420a-a187-694d6e1ed1dd'::uuid,'17ee7349c727cf40cd528cf2fa3def7f','Termite predators near the circles only confirms that termites live there, which the evidence already told us. Being present at the scene isn''t the same as causing the circles, so this adds nothing to the causal claim.'),
('e5b5f4a7-fae1-49de-91bf-948279747b6f'::uuid,'e32cb3e2701372002049d5d208e3e49a','This rules out the two best scavenger stories. If the poles weren''t for "self-defense" and weren''t used to "cut or transport scavenged carcasses", hunting becomes the likeliest use for sharpened poles found next to horse remains. It doesn''t prove the precursors hunted, but it closes the gap the argument left open.

The move: when the evidence is a set of objects and the conclusion is about what they were used for, strengthen by eliminating the other uses.'),
('6f9c17da-3b3c-4967-b6f8-ce25f4114dbc'::uuid,'4e0ed0e30109251973d7f8ec446b6c70','This says people in the area used stones and sticks as tools. The argument already assumes the precursors made the tools. The open question is what the tools were for, and B doesn''t say anything about hunting.'),
('90c9893a-c88e-41df-9e6a-ce497ee07a83'::uuid,'08e0f445281e8ef7be7d2d87759082c8','C is about when Homo sapiens evolved, but the premise that matters is about when they lived in Europe, and evolving earlier doesn''t put them in Europe earlier. If it did anything, it would open the possibility of Homo sapiens having made the poles, which hurts the argument. Either way, it says nothing about whether the poles were used to hunt.'),
('5cc88343-dfab-4769-ab56-ea7d9f7dd46a'::uuid,'6f3faac1bfed7f56556ecfb885a523a2','Behavior that varied across ecosystems tells us nothing about what this group in northern Europe did. Divergent behavior could include hunting or not, so D gives us no reason to think these poles were hunting tools.'),
('a8404e68-54a7-48cf-8507-63366504ea32'::uuid,'77ba54dcff73182e474e24ad9740eb84','This is about Homo sapiens who already hunted, not about the precursors. Knowing that later hunters kept scavenging and gathering gives no evidence that the precursors hunted at all, which is what the conclusion needs.'),
('445b6fca-7df8-49df-9f51-95244c54ab53'::uuid,'428d29dd8186e2763257aa6752fa3b31','A recent rise in organic farming doesn''t tell us why farms switched or whether taste earns anything. Ask the test question: does this connect taste to revenue? It doesn''t, so it leaves the gap where it was.'),
('dd12d76a-7952-46e4-88ef-d4bdbd2ffecd'::uuid,'67cd25b418a60c8fa8ec0dcaac09b363','This links short-run profit to long-run profit, but we know nothing about organic orchards'' short-run profits. With no information to plug in, B can''t move the conclusion. The gap is between taste and money, not between time periods.'),
('7d0460f9-53b3-48c2-9c79-cdfb2295d084'::uuid,'42d58317acb47a8bfb8d147ec5d8095f','This is the prediction. If demand for apples is "based primarily on flavor", better tasting organic apples will be in higher demand, which means more sales or better prices. With costs and output already equal, more revenue means more profit.

The move: when the evidence holds everything equal except one difference, the right strengthener shows that the one difference matters to the outcome in the conclusion.'),
('60124784-d5cc-439d-b064-a275f8384cff'::uuid,'abf8caa7006efb760f5e5f246b99b7fc','If most people buy the apples with the "lowest price", taste isn''t what drives sales. Then better tasting organic apples wouldn''t earn more, which pushes against the argument rather than for it.'),
('46491d84-f8b9-4c82-845f-40e14d445868'::uuid,'853297c53b4072b27c5bf33a01f9e1e3','This gives conventional apples an advantage in appearance. If buyers care about looks, conventional orchards might sell more, which cuts against the conclusion. It certainly doesn''t connect better taste to more profit.'),
('cad3bc58-30f8-46b4-bd96-c44eda3d7dd0'::uuid,'7ce7fbe69c8543647f2ef5c5b758fc33','Creativity isn''t adventurousness, and a few famous first-borns don''t affect a claim about how first-borns tend to compare with their siblings. If anything, these are small counterexamples, so A can''t help the argument.'),
('0f72952f-3234-4590-a999-2e62bc9da3c6'::uuid,'9161447fae39dd9e52840e6c3d712cdb','A first-born has more younger siblings than anyone else in the family. So if "the more younger siblings one has" means more plasticity, first-borns should be the most plastic, the opposite of what the study found. B cuts against the evidence and says nothing about adventure.'),
('8c287a05-313d-4912-90ac-6d6fdedb408e'::uuid,'81c1fe9a4ce286f10af85ae40b1b6ed4','This shores up the weak bridge. The psychologist only said it was reasonable to link openness with adventure. C says other studies found that cognitive plasticity goes with "willingness to take risks", and taking risks is a clear form of being adventurous. Now the study''s finding about first-borns connects to the conclusion.

The move: when a premise is introduced as a hunch, the strengthener is often outside evidence that the hunch is true.'),
('fee41ea0-9960-4d67-9405-01fc92a7d907'::uuid,'b5b9fc886c6d20b56ac245ee4a506445','Knowing that "several industry leaders have older siblings" gives us a tiny, narrow sample, and we''re never told how many first-borns lead companies too. Running a business also isn''t shown to be adventurous. Nothing here connects openness to new ideas with adventure.'),
('f2fd641a-c78d-438d-b796-10441da62880'::uuid,'acbf2b5d1c70cdc91a926047126df337','Participants described themselves as "more adaptable than other people." That''s true of most of the group, first-borns included, so it doesn''t separate first-borns from their siblings. And adaptable isn''t the same as adventurous.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;